==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|neoterical}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|neotericus}}, from Hellenistic Greek {{m|grc|νεωτερικός}}, from comparative of {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|νέος||new}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/niːə(ʊ)ˈtɛɹɪk/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Modern, [[new-fangled]].
#* '''1873''', Fitzedward Hall, ''Modern English'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=Fxo_AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA294&dq=%22our+neoteric+verbs%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjHyPPw6fvlAhUyWN8KHcnvAtIQ6AEwAXoECAgQAg#v=onepage&q=%22our%20neoteric%20verbs%22&f=false page 294]
#*: Among our '''neoteric''' verbs, those in ''-ize'' are exceedingly numerous.
# New; recent.
#* '''1998''', ''The Toronto Star'', August 21
#*: Should it all come crashing in on us . . . will there be enough luddites, whose hands remember, to free us from the chains of '''neoteric''' technology?
#* '''1997''', Espen Aarseth, ''Cybertext''
#*: A few words on the two '''neoteric''' terms, ''[[cybertext]]'' and ''[[ergodic]]'', are in order.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A modern author (especially as opposed to a classical writer).
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy}}, Bk.I, New York, 2001, p.140:
#*: Galen himself writes promiscuously of them both by reason of their affinity; but most of our '''neoterics''' do handle them apart, whom I will follow in this treatise.
# Someone with new or modern ideas.
# {{lb|en|historical}} any poet who belonged to the [[neoterics]], a series of avant-garde Latin poets who wrote in the 1st century BC such as [[Catullus]], {{W|Helvius Cinna}}, {{W|Publius Valerius Cato}}, {{W|Marcus Furius Bibaculus}} and {{W|Quintus Cornificius}}.

